WEBVTT

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:03.348
Imagine a parts tray that never reaches the line.

00:00:03.665 --> 00:00:04.987
Now the line is waiting,

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:07.347
the radio's going, and you're the one

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:08.680
who has to explain it.

00:00:09.255 --> 00:00:11.927
That's the problem behind Witness Fleet.

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:13.707
It connects the stopped job to the evidence

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:16.755
and the permission needed to continue.

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:19.053
If a release is missing, the next move waits.

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:21.175
You get a record you can follow,

00:00:21.208 --> 00:00:23.244
instead of a story you have to piece together.

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:26.039
We've built the first version in simulation.

00:00:26.447 --> 00:00:28.860
Three robots work through a blocked delivery.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:30.717
The release is scripted;

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:32.915
the run records the completed delivery.

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:35.687
Picture the same headache in a warehouse aisle,

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:37.525
or a hospital supply corridor.

00:00:37.810 --> 00:00:40.058
We'd start with one recurring problem at one site.

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:43.276
Measure the time people spend untangling it.

00:00:43.679 --> 00:00:46.540
That's where this product has to earn its place.

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:48.247
Witness Fleet.

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:49.509
So the next time someone asks

00:00:49.542 --> 00:00:51.797
what happened, you have somewhere to start.
